# FACT 4, the regression fixture. hledger reads checking.csv with these rules,
# exits 0, and produces garbage.
#
# Plausible provenance: rules written for a deposits-only feed from the same
# bank. They survive stage 1 — four fields against four columns, and %m/%d/%Y
# really does read this file's dates — so nothing cheap can tell them apart from
# checking.csv.rules. Only hledger's structured output can.
#
# The empty third name means "ignore column 3", so `amount` reads column 4, the
# Deposit column. Four of the five rows are withdrawals with an empty Deposit
# cell, and each of those becomes a transaction whose single posting has NO
# AMOUNT AT ALL. The fifth has no if-rule to categorise it, so hledger falls back
# to income:unknown.
#
# `hledger check` is perfectly happy with all of it. That is the point: parse
# success is not a matching signal.

skip 1
fields date, description, , amount
date-format %m/%d/%Y
currency $

account1 assets:bank:checking
